export default [
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A startup is launching a new mobile app and has no idea whether it will get 1,000 or 1,000,000 users. Instead of buying servers sized for the worst case, it plans to add and remove capacity as real demand appears. Which advantage of cloud computing does this describe?","o":["Benefit from massive economies of scale","Go global in minutes","Stop guessing capacity","Stop spending money running and maintaining data centers"],"a":[2],"e":"Stop guessing capacity means you scale up or down based on actual demand instead of forecasting and overprovisioning. Economies of scale explains AWS's lower unit prices, not how much capacity you provision."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Developers at a company used to wait six weeks for IT to procure test servers. On AWS, they can create a test environment in minutes and delete it when finished. Which advantage of cloud computing does this describe?","o":["Trade fixed expense for variable expense","Benefit from massive economies of scale","Go global in minutes","Increase speed and agility"],"a":[3],"e":"Increase speed and agility means new resources are a click away, cutting the time to get resources from weeks to minutes. Trading fixed for variable expense is about the payment model, not how fast resources become available."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A European e-commerce company wants to give customers in Japan low-latency access to its application. It deploys a copy of its stack in the Asia Pacific (Tokyo) Region in a single afternoon. Which advantage of cloud computing does this demonstrate?","o":["Stop guessing capacity","Go global in minutes","Benefit from massive economies of scale","Trade fixed expense for variable expense"],"a":[1],"e":"Go global in minutes means you can deploy in multiple Regions around the world with a few clicks, giving users lower latency at minimal cost. Stop guessing capacity is about sizing resources, not geographic reach."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's IT staff spend much of their time racking servers, replacing failed disks, and managing data center cooling. After moving to AWS, they focus on building features for customers instead. Which advantage of cloud computing is this?","o":["Stop spending money running and maintaining data centers","Increase speed and agility","Go global in minutes","Benefit from massive economies of scale"],"a":[0],"e":"Stop spending money running and maintaining data centers means you focus on your customers instead of the heavy lifting of racking, stacking, and powering servers. Speed and agility is about how fast you get resources, not about who maintains the facilities."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"An online retailer's traffic increases tenfold during a two-week holiday sale and returns to normal afterward. Which characteristic of the AWS Cloud lets the retailer handle the peak without paying for idle servers the rest of the year?","o":["Durability","Elasticity","Fault isolation","Data residency"],"a":[1],"e":"Elasticity automatically adds resources when demand rises and removes them when it falls, so you pay only for capacity you use. Durability is about not losing stored data, not about matching capacity to demand."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's application must stay available to users even if an entire AWS Region becomes unavailable. What should the company do?","o":["Deploy the application across multiple Availability Zones in one Region","Use larger Amazon EC2 instance types","Deploy the application in multiple AWS Regions","Run the application compute on edge locations"],"a":[2],"e":"Only a multi-Region deployment survives the loss of a whole Region. Multiple Availability Zones protect against the failure of a data center or an AZ, but they are all inside one Region."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A research team wants to try a machine learning prototype. If it does not work, they want to shut everything down with almost no money lost. Which cloud benefit makes this low-risk experimentation possible?","o":["High availability across Availability Zones","Global reach through edge locations","Data durability in Amazon S3","Agility, because resources can be provisioned and released quickly at low cost"],"a":[3],"e":"Agility in the cloud lowers the cost of experimentation: you provision resources quickly, test ideas, and release resources when done. High availability keeps a workload running during failures but does not reduce the cost of trying new ideas."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A healthcare company expanding into Germany must keep patient data stored inside the European Union. Which AWS capability helps the company meet this requirement?","o":["Caching the data in Amazon CloudFront edge locations","Choosing an AWS Region in the EU, because data stays in the Region the customer selects unless the customer moves it","Using AWS Local Zones in the United States","Enabling Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration"],"a":[1],"e":"Customers choose the Region where their data is stored, and AWS does not move it out of that Region without the customer's action, which supports data residency. Edge locations cache content worldwide, so they do not keep data inside a specific jurisdiction."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A CFO asks how moving to AWS changes the way IT spending appears in the company's finances. Which answer is correct?","o":["Spending shifts from operational expenditure (OpEx) to capital expenditure (CapEx)","All IT costs disappear because AWS absorbs them","Spending shifts from capital expenditure (CapEx) to operational expenditure (OpEx)","The company must prepay three years of usage as a capital purchase"],"a":[2],"e":"Instead of large upfront hardware purchases (CapEx), the company pays for IT resources as it consumes them (OpEx). Cloud does not remove IT costs, and prepayment is optional (for example, Savings Plans), not required."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company is building a business case for moving to AWS. Which statements describe cost benefits of the AWS Cloud? (Choose TWO.)","o":["The company receives a fixed monthly bill regardless of usage","Pay-as-you-go pricing replaces large upfront hardware purchases","The company no longer pays for any IT staff","Lower prices result from AWS aggregating usage across many customers","Data transfer is always free in every direction"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Pay-as-you-go pricing trades fixed expense for variable expense, and AWS's economies of scale lower unit prices. Bills vary with usage, the company still needs IT staff for its own responsibilities, and data transfer out of AWS is charged."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to deliver videos and images with low latency to users in dozens of countries without deploying servers in each country. Which part of the AWS global infrastructure supports this?","o":["Edge locations used by Amazon CloudFront","Additional Availability Zones in one Region","AWS Outposts racks in each country","A larger Amazon EC2 instance in one Region"],"a":[0],"e":"CloudFront caches content at a worldwide network of edge locations close to users, giving global reach without servers in each country. Extra Availability Zones improve availability inside one Region but do not bring content closer to users in other countries."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which statements describe how the AWS Cloud increases business agility? (Choose TWO.)","o":["New IT resources are only a few clicks away","Hardware must be ordered weeks in advance","Teams can experiment at low cost and discard what does not work","Capacity must be committed for several years before use","All workloads must run from a single data center"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Agility comes from getting resources in minutes and from the low cost of failure, which encourages experimentation. Long procurement lead times and mandatory multi-year commitments are characteristics of traditional on-premises IT, not AWS."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company runs a reporting cluster that is needed for only three days at the end of each month. How can the AWS Cloud reduce the cost of this workload?","o":["Buy the servers once and keep them running all month","Purchase Dedicated Hosts for the whole year","Move the cluster to a single larger instance that runs continuously","Launch the cluster at month-end and terminate it afterward, paying only for the hours used"],"a":[3],"e":"With pay-as-you-go pricing you can create resources when needed and terminate them when done, turning a fixed cost into a variable one. Keeping servers running all month pays for capacity that sits idle most of the time."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A mobile game's user base grows steadily over a year, and the company adds more Amazon EC2 instances behind a load balancer to handle the increasing load. Which concept does this best describe?","o":["Durability","Scalability","Data residency","Fault isolation"],"a":[1],"e":"Scalability is a system's ability to grow to handle increased load, for example by adding instances. Durability is about protecting stored data from loss, not handling more users."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company needs to launch an identical copy of its production environment for a new market within one day. It already defines all its infrastructure in AWS CloudFormation templates. Which benefit makes this fast deployment possible?","o":["Economies of scale lower the unit price of resources","Edge locations cache the environment for faster launch","Infrastructure as code lets the company deploy repeatable environments quickly through automation","Dedicated Hosts guarantee capacity in the new Region"],"a":[2],"e":"Defining infrastructure as code lets you deploy the same environment repeatedly, quickly, and consistently in any Region. Economies of scale affect price, not how fast an environment can be created."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A security architect requires that every person and workload use least-privilege permissions, that identity management be centralized, and that long-term static credentials be eliminated. Which Well-Architected Security design principle is this?","o":["Apply security at all layers","Implement a strong identity foundation","Maintain traceability","Keep people away from data"],"a":[1],"e":"Implement a strong identity foundation means least privilege, separation of duties, centralized identity management, and avoiding long-term static credentials. Maintain traceability is about logging and monitoring actions, not granting permissions."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company logs all API activity and configuration changes in its environment and automatically alerts the security team when unexpected changes occur. Which Well-Architected Security design principle does this follow?","o":["Protect data in transit and at rest","Automate security best practices","Prepare for security events","Maintain traceability"],"a":[3],"e":"Maintain traceability means monitoring, alerting, and auditing actions and changes in real time and integrating logs with systems that respond automatically. Prepare for security events is about incident response practice, not continuous logging."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"To reduce the risk of mishandling sensitive data, a company replaces manual database queries by employees with automated reports and dashboards, so that people no longer access the raw data directly. Which Well-Architected Security design principle is this?","o":["Keep people away from data","Implement a strong identity foundation","Apply security at all layers","Automate security best practices"],"a":[0],"e":"Keep people away from data means using tools and automation to reduce or eliminate direct human access to data, lowering the risk of mishandling or human error. A strong identity foundation controls who has permissions, but this scenario removes the need for direct access entirely."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's security team regularly runs simulated incident response exercises and uses automated tools to speed up detection, investigation, and recovery. Which Well-Architected Security design principle does this follow?","o":["Apply security at all layers","Maintain traceability","Prepare for security events","Keep people away from data"],"a":[2],"e":"Prepare for security events means having incident management processes and running simulations to practice detection and recovery. Maintain traceability provides the logs used during an incident, but it does not cover rehearsing the response."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company protects its application with AWS WAF at the edge, network ACLs on subnets, security groups on instances, operating system hardening, and input validation in the code. Which Well-Architected Security design principle does this defense-in-depth approach follow?","o":["Implement a strong identity foundation","Apply security at all layers","Maintain traceability","Protect data in transit and at rest"],"a":[1],"e":"Apply security at all layers means using defense in depth with multiple controls at every layer: edge, VPC, subnet, instance, operating system, and application. Protecting data in transit and at rest is about encryption, not layered network and host controls."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company defines its security controls, such as encryption settings and network rules, in version-controlled templates so that every new environment is deployed with the same secure configuration. Which Well-Architected Security design principle is this?","o":["Keep people away from data","Prepare for security events","Maintain traceability","Automate security best practices"],"a":[3],"e":"Automate security best practices means implementing security controls as code in version-controlled templates so they are applied consistently and scale quickly. Maintain traceability is about logging and monitoring, not about deploying controls."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A team regularly injects failures into a test copy of its workload, such as stopping instances, to confirm that the workload recovers as expected before a real failure happens. This aligns with a design principle of which Well-Architected pillar?","o":["Reliability","Security","Cost Optimization","Performance Efficiency"],"a":[0],"e":"Test recovery procedures is a Reliability design principle: in the cloud you can simulate failures to validate recovery strategies. Performance Efficiency focuses on using resources efficiently, not on proving that a workload recovers."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company replaces one very large Amazon EC2 instance with several smaller instances spread across Availability Zones, so that the failure of one instance affects only a small part of the traffic. Which Well-Architected design principle is this?","o":["Use serverless architectures","Experiment more often","Scale horizontally to increase aggregate workload availability","Adopt a consumption model"],"a":[2],"e":"Scaling horizontally replaces one large resource with multiple small ones to reduce the impact of a single failure, a Reliability design principle. Experiment more often is a Performance Efficiency principle about testing different configurations."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A workload monitors key performance indicators and, when a threshold is breached, automatically replaces unhealthy resources without human intervention. Which Well-Architected Reliability design principle is this?","o":["Stop guessing capacity","Test recovery procedures","Manage change through automation","Automatically recover from failure"],"a":[3],"e":"Automatically recover from failure means monitoring KPIs and triggering automation when thresholds are breached, sometimes even before a failure occurs. Test recovery procedures is about simulating failures to validate recovery, not reacting automatically in production."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A small company with no machine learning experts adds image recognition to its app by calling a managed AWS AI service instead of building and training its own models. Which Well-Architected Performance Efficiency design principle is this?","o":["Consider mechanical sympathy","Democratize advanced technologies","Maximize utilization","Manage change through automation"],"a":[1],"e":"Democratize advanced technologies means consuming complex technologies as services from the cloud provider so your team does not need deep expertise. Mechanical sympathy is about choosing a technology that matches how you access data, not about outsourcing expertise."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A team builds a new application with AWS Lambda and Amazon S3 so that it never has to run or maintain servers for traditional compute activities. Which Well-Architected Performance Efficiency design principle is this?","o":["Use serverless architectures","Go global in minutes","Experiment more often","Scale horizontally to increase aggregate workload availability"],"a":[0],"e":"Use serverless architectures removes the need to run and maintain physical servers for compute. Scale horizontally is a Reliability principle about using many small resources to reduce the impact of a failure."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Because virtual resources are easy to create, a team runs quick comparative tests of several instance types and storage configurations before choosing one for production. Which Well-Architected Performance Efficiency design principle is this?","o":["Democratize advanced technologies","Use serverless architectures","Experiment more often","Implement cloud financial management"],"a":[2],"e":"Experiment more often means using virtual and automatable resources to quickly carry out comparative testing with different configurations. Democratize advanced technologies is about consuming complex technologies as services, not comparing configurations."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"An architect chooses a key-value database for a workload that performs millions of simple lookups by key, instead of a relational database, because it matches how the data is accessed. Which Well-Architected Performance Efficiency design principle is this?","o":["Go global in minutes","Experiment more often","Democratize advanced technologies","Consider mechanical sympathy"],"a":[3],"e":"Consider mechanical sympathy means using the technology approach that best aligns with your goals, for example considering data access patterns when choosing a database. Experiment more often is about comparative testing, not about matching technology to access patterns."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company stops its development and test environments outside business hours, cutting their cost by about 75%. Which Well-Architected Cost Optimization design principle is this?","o":["Measure overall efficiency","Adopt a consumption model","Analyze and attribute expenditure","Maximize utilization"],"a":[1],"e":"Adopt a consumption model means paying only for the resources you need and scaling with business requirements, such as stopping dev and test environments when not in use. Maximize utilization is a Sustainability principle about reducing idle resources for environmental impact."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company moves from self-managed database servers to Amazon RDS so its staff no longer spend time on hardware and routine database administration, and it redirects that money to customer features. Which Well-Architected Cost Optimization design principle is this?","o":["Measure overall efficiency","Implement cloud financial management","Stop spending money on undifferentiated heavy lifting","Analyze and attribute expenditure"],"a":[2],"e":"Stop spending money on undifferentiated heavy lifting means letting AWS handle infrastructure work such as racking servers and managing databases so you focus on customers. Implement cloud financial management is about building cost-management capability and processes, not offloading operational work."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company tags all resources by business unit and product so that it can see which products generate cloud costs and measure the return on each investment. Which Well-Architected Cost Optimization design principle is this?","o":["Adopt a consumption model","Stop spending money on undifferentiated heavy lifting","Measure overall efficiency","Analyze and attribute expenditure"],"a":[3],"e":"Analyze and attribute expenditure means accurately identifying the cost and usage of workloads and attributing IT costs to revenue streams and owners. Adopt a consumption model is about paying only for what you use, not allocating costs to owners."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company tracks the cost of each order its online store processes and works to reduce that cost while keeping output the same. Which Well-Architected Cost Optimization design principle is this?","o":["Analyze and attribute expenditure","Measure overall efficiency","Adopt a consumption model","Maximize utilization"],"a":[1],"e":"Measure overall efficiency means measuring the business output of a workload and the cost of delivering it, then using that data to improve. Analyze and attribute expenditure focuses on assigning costs to owners rather than measuring cost per unit of business output."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company creates a dedicated team and processes so that finance and technology groups work together on cloud cost awareness, budgeting, and forecasting. Which Well-Architected Cost Optimization design principle is this?","o":["Implement cloud financial management","Measure overall efficiency","Organize teams around business outcomes","Stop spending money on undifferentiated heavy lifting"],"a":[0],"e":"Implement cloud financial management means investing time and resources to build capability, knowledge, and programs for cost management. Organize teams around business outcomes is an Operational Excellence principle, not a cost management practice."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company consolidates dozens of Amazon EC2 instances that each run at 10% CPU onto fewer, well-utilized instances, mainly to reduce the energy its workload consumes. Which Well-Architected Sustainability design principle is this?","o":["Scale horizontally to increase aggregate workload availability","Maximize utilization","Go global in minutes","Experiment more often"],"a":[1],"e":"Maximize utilization means right-sizing and consolidating so that resources run at high utilization, reducing the energy consumed by idle capacity. Scaling horizontally is a Reliability principle aimed at availability, not at reducing energy use."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"To reduce the energy used per unit of work, a company plans to move its workloads to newer AWS Graviton-based instance types as they become available. Which Well-Architected Sustainability design principle is this?","o":["Understand your impact","Reduce the downstream impact of your cloud workloads","Anticipate and adopt new, more efficient hardware and software offerings","Use serverless architectures"],"a":[2],"e":"Anticipate and adopt new, more efficient hardware and software offerings means monitoring and adopting more efficient options, such as newer instance families. Reducing downstream impact is about the energy your customers' devices use to consume your service."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company optimizes its web application so that customers' phones and laptops need less processing power and fewer device upgrades to use it. Which Well-Architected Sustainability design principle is this?","o":["Maximize utilization","Use managed services","Establish sustainability goals","Reduce the downstream impact of your cloud workloads"],"a":[3],"e":"Reduce the downstream impact of your cloud workloads means lowering the energy and resources your customers need to use your service, such as reducing the need for device upgrades. Maximize utilization is about your own resources in AWS, not customers' devices."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company sets a long-term target to reduce the compute resources required per transaction by 30% and models the return on investment for meeting it. Which Well-Architected Sustainability design principle is this?","o":["Establish sustainability goals","Measure overall efficiency","Adopt a consumption model","Anticipate and adopt new, more efficient hardware and software offerings"],"a":[0],"e":"Establish sustainability goals means setting long-term targets for each workload, such as reducing resources required per transaction, and modeling ROI. Measure overall efficiency is a Cost Optimization principle focused on cost per business output."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company is choosing between two AWS Regions that both meet its latency and compliance requirements. It selects the one with a lower carbon intensity of electricity. Which Well-Architected pillar most directly guides this decision?","o":["Cost Optimization","Performance Efficiency","Sustainability","Reliability"],"a":[2],"e":"The Sustainability pillar recommends choosing Regions based on both business requirements and sustainability goals, such as lower carbon intensity. Cost Optimization would choose a Region based on price, not environmental impact."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"An operations team collects metrics, logs, and traces from its workload so it can understand the workload's behavior, spot issues early, and make data-driven decisions. Which Well-Architected Operational Excellence design principle is this?","o":["Maintain traceability","Implement observability for actionable insights","Test recovery procedures","Consider mechanical sympathy"],"a":[1],"e":"Implement observability for actionable insights is an Operational Excellence principle about understanding workload behavior and performance through telemetry. Maintain traceability is a Security principle focused on auditing actions and changes, not on overall workload health."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company restructures its operations so that each team owns a set of business outcomes and aligns its operational priorities with them. Which Well-Architected Operational Excellence design principle is this?","o":["Implement cloud financial management","Make frequent, small, reversible changes","Organize teams around business outcomes","Refine operations procedures frequently"],"a":[2],"e":"Organize teams around business outcomes is an Operational Excellence principle that aligns team structure, leadership, and culture with business results. Implement cloud financial management is a Cost Optimization principle about cost awareness."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"An operations team reviews and updates its runbooks every time the workload changes, and holds regular reviews to validate that the procedures are still effective and that the team is familiar with them. Which Well-Architected Operational Excellence design principle is this?","o":["Test recovery procedures","Prepare for security events","Experiment more often","Refine operations procedures frequently"],"a":[3],"e":"Refine operations procedures frequently means evolving your procedures as the workload evolves, looking for chances to improve them, and holding regular reviews to validate that they are effective. Test recovery procedures is a Reliability principle about validating that the workload recovers from failure."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Because environments can be created on demand, a team builds a full production-scale copy of its system, runs a load test, and deletes the environment afterward, paying only for the test duration. Which Well-Architected general design principle is this?","o":["Test systems at production scale","Stop guessing capacity","Use serverless architectures","Maximize utilization"],"a":[0],"e":"Test systems at production scale is a general design principle: in the cloud you can create a production-scale test environment on demand and decommission it afterward. Stop guessing capacity is about sizing production, not testing."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which are design principles of the Security pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Scale horizontally to increase aggregate workload availability","Apply security at all layers","Adopt a consumption model","Protect data in transit and at rest","Use serverless architectures"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Apply security at all layers and protect data in transit and at rest are Security design principles. Scaling horizontally is a Reliability principle, adopting a consumption model is Cost Optimization, and serverless architectures is Performance Efficiency."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which are design principles of the Reliability pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Democratize advanced technologies","Keep people away from data","Automatically recover from failure","Maximize utilization","Stop guessing capacity"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Automatically recover from failure and stop guessing capacity are Reliability design principles. Democratize advanced technologies is Performance Efficiency, keep people away from data is Security, and maximize utilization is Sustainability."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which are design principles of the Performance Efficiency pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Go global in minutes","Implement a strong identity foundation","Analyze and attribute expenditure","Use serverless architectures","Test recovery procedures"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Go global in minutes and use serverless architectures are Performance Efficiency design principles. A strong identity foundation is Security, analyzing expenditure is Cost Optimization, and testing recovery procedures is Reliability."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which are design principles of the Cost Optimization pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Measure overall efficiency","Automatically recover from failure","Adopt a consumption model","Consider mechanical sympathy","Maintain traceability"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Measure overall efficiency and adopt a consumption model are Cost Optimization design principles. Automatic recovery is Reliability, mechanical sympathy is Performance Efficiency, and traceability is Security."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A solutions architect wants to review a workload against AWS architectural best practices by answering a set of questions, track progress with milestones, and generate an improvement plan, at no additional charge. Which AWS service should the architect use?","o":["AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Config","AWS Pricing Calculator"],"a":[1],"e":"The AWS Well-Architected Tool is a free console service for reviewing workloads against the Framework's pillars, recording milestones, and producing improvement plans. Trusted Advisor runs automated checks on account resources but does not run a question-based workload review."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company runs a serverless application and wants its Well-Architected review to include best practices specific to serverless workloads. What should it do?","o":["Use AWS Artifact to download a serverless compliance report","Enable AWS Trusted Advisor serverless checks","Run a review against the AWS CAF Platform perspective","Apply the Serverless Applications Lens in the AWS Well-Architected Tool"],"a":[3],"e":"Lenses extend the Well-Architected review with technology- or industry-specific guidance, and the Serverless Applications Lens is available in the Well-Architected Tool. AWS CAF guides cloud adoption at the organization level, not workload architecture reviews."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A manager believes that completing an AWS Well-Architected review will certify the company's workload as PCI DSS compliant. Which statement is correct?","o":["The review produces an official PCI DSS certificate signed by AWS","The review automatically fixes all high-risk issues","The review helps identify risks against architectural best practices, but it is not a compliance certification","The review is only available to customers with AWS Enterprise Support"],"a":[2],"e":"The Well-Architected Framework is a set of best practices that helps you find and fix architectural risks; it does not certify compliance. The Well-Architected Tool is available to all customers, not only Enterprise Support customers."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's IT team builds a 50-node cluster for a project, but monitoring shows the instances average 5% CPU utilization. The team switches to smaller instance types to reduce cost. Which Well-Architected pillar does this change primarily address?","o":["Cost Optimization","Reliability","Security","Operational Excellence"],"a":[0],"e":"Right-sizing underused resources to lower spend is a core Cost Optimization practice. Reliability is about recovering from failure and meeting demand, which this change does not target."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's leadership wants AWS guidance to identify and prioritize cloud transformation opportunities, evaluate its cloud readiness, and evolve its transformation roadmap over time. Which AWS resource best fits?","o":["AWS Well-Architected Framework","AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)","AWS shared responsibility model","AWS Pricing Calculator"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS CAF uses AWS experience to help organizations plan and accelerate cloud transformation, assess readiness, and build a roadmap. The Well-Architected Framework evaluates the architecture of individual workloads, not organization-wide adoption."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"According to the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), which are business outcomes that cloud transformation can improve? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Elimination of all security responsibilities for the customer","Guaranteed zero downtime for all workloads","Reduced business risk","Removal of the need for any IT staff","Improved environmental, social, and governance (ESG) performance"],"a":[2,4],"e":"AWS CAF lists four business outcomes: reduced business risk, improved ESG performance, increased revenue, and increased operational efficiency. Customers keep security responsibilities under the shared responsibility model, and no architecture guarantees zero downtime."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants guidance on building an enterprise-grade, scalable hybrid cloud platform, modernizing existing workloads, and implementing CI/CD pipelines. Which AWS CAF perspective focuses on these capabilities?","o":["Operations","Business","Governance","Platform"],"a":[3],"e":"The Platform perspective covers platform architecture and engineering, data architecture, provisioning and orchestration, modern application development, and CI/CD. Operations focuses on running and supporting cloud services once they are delivered."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which AWS CAF perspective helps an organization achieve the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of its data and cloud workloads, with capabilities such as threat detection, identity and access management, and incident response?","o":["Governance","Security","Operations","Platform"],"a":[1],"e":"The Security perspective covers security governance, identity and access management, threat detection, data protection, and incident response. Governance covers risk and financial management in general, not security controls."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to make sure its cloud services are delivered at a level that meets business needs, using capabilities such as observability, event management, incident and problem management, and patch management. Which AWS CAF perspective is this?","o":["Platform","People","Operations","Business"],"a":[2],"e":"The Operations perspective ensures cloud services are run and supported to agreed levels, with capabilities such as observability, incident management, and patch management. Platform focuses on building the cloud environment rather than operating it day to day."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to make sure its cloud investments accelerate its digital transformation and business outcomes, using capabilities such as strategy management, portfolio management, and data monetization. Which AWS CAF perspective is this?","o":["Business","Governance","People","Platform"],"a":[0],"e":"The Business perspective ensures cloud investments drive business outcomes, with capabilities such as strategy, portfolio, product, and innovation management and data monetization. Governance orchestrates initiatives and manages risk and cost, not business strategy."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"In an AWS CAF engagement, the common stakeholders for one perspective include the chief information security officer (CISO), the chief compliance officer, and internal audit leaders. Which perspective is this?","o":["Governance","Operations","Business","Security"],"a":[3],"e":"Security perspective stakeholders typically include the CISO, chief compliance officer, internal audit leaders, and security architects. Governance stakeholders include the chief transformation officer, CIO, CTO, CFO, chief data officer, and chief risk officer."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"In an AWS CAF engagement, the common stakeholders for one perspective include infrastructure and operations leaders, site reliability engineers, and IT service managers. Which perspective is this?","o":["Platform","Operations","People","Security"],"a":[1],"e":"Operations perspective stakeholders are the people who run and support cloud services: infrastructure and operations leaders, SREs, and IT service managers. Platform stakeholders are technology leaders, architects, and engineers who build the environment."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's HR and leadership teams want to evolve the company culture, build cloud fluency across the workforce, and redesign the organization to support new ways of working. Which AWS CAF perspective covers these capabilities?","o":["Business","Governance","People","Operations"],"a":[2],"e":"The People perspective includes culture evolution, transformational leadership, cloud fluency, workforce transformation, and organization design. Governance focuses on program, risk, and financial management, not culture and skills."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to orchestrate its cloud initiatives while maximizing organizational benefits and minimizing transformation risks, including tracking the business benefits of each initiative. Which AWS CAF perspective covers this?","o":["Governance","Business","Platform","Security"],"a":[0],"e":"The Governance perspective orchestrates cloud initiatives with capabilities such as program management, benefits management, risk management, and cloud financial management. Business focuses on strategy and business outcomes rather than oversight and risk controls."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which are perspectives of the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Reliability","Platform","Sustainability","Operations","Cost Optimization"],"a":[1,3],"e":"The six AWS CAF perspectives are Business, People, Governance, Platform, Security, and Operations. Reliability, Sustainability, and Cost Optimization are pillars of the Well-Architected Framework, not CAF perspectives."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"In AWS CAF, migrating and modernizing legacy infrastructure, applications, and data platforms belongs to which transformation domain?","o":["Process","Organization","Product","Technology"],"a":[3],"e":"The Technology domain focuses on using the cloud to migrate and modernize legacy infrastructure, applications, and data and analytics platforms. The Process domain is about digitizing and automating business operations."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company uses cloud-based data analytics and machine learning to digitize, automate, and optimize its business operations, such as order processing. Which AWS CAF transformation domain is this?","o":["Technology","Process","Product","Organization"],"a":[1],"e":"The Process domain focuses on digitizing, automating, and optimizing business operations, often with data, analytics, and ML. The Technology domain is about migrating and modernizing the underlying IT platforms."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company reimagines its operating model by organizing teams around products and value streams instead of around technology functions. Which AWS CAF transformation domain is this?","o":["Product","Technology","Organization","Process"],"a":[2],"e":"The Organization domain focuses on reimagining the operating model, including how teams are organized around products and value streams. The Product domain is about creating new value propositions and revenue models."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A bank uses the cloud to launch new digital products and create new revenue models that were not possible before. Which AWS CAF transformation domain is this?","o":["Product","Process","Organization","Technology"],"a":[0],"e":"The Product domain focuses on reimagining the business model by creating new value propositions (products and services) and revenue models. The Process domain improves existing business operations rather than creating new offerings."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"In the AWS CAF cloud transformation journey (Envision, Align, Launch, Scale), during which phase does a company deliver pilot initiatives in production and demonstrate incremental business value?","o":["Envision","Align","Launch","Scale"],"a":[2],"e":"In the Launch phase you deliver pilot initiatives in production and show incremental value. Scale comes afterward, expanding successful pilots to the desired breadth and business impact."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"During portfolio discovery before a migration, a company finds that 15% of its applications are no longer used by anyone. What is the most appropriate migration strategy for these applications?","o":["Retain","Rehost","Retire","Relocate"],"a":[2],"e":"Retire means decommissioning applications that are no longer useful, which cuts cost and reduces the migration scope. Retain means keeping applications in the source environment because they are still needed but not ready to move."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company has an application that needs very low latency to factory equipment on site and recently received a major on-premises upgrade. The company decides to keep it where it is for now and revisit the decision later. Which migration strategy is this?","o":["Retire","Retain","Repurchase","Rehost"],"a":[1],"e":"Retain (revisit) means keeping an application in the source environment for now, for example because it depends on on-site equipment such as factory machines or was recently upgraded, and reconsidering later. Retire would decommission the application, but this one is still needed."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company moves hundreds of VMware vSphere virtual machines to a VMware environment running on AWS at the hypervisor level, without buying new hardware, rewriting applications, or changing its existing operations. Which migration strategy is this?","o":["Rehost","Replatform","Refactor","Relocate"],"a":[3],"e":"Relocate moves a large number of servers at once from an on-premises platform to a cloud version of the same platform (for example, a VMware environment on AWS such as Amazon Elastic VMware Service) without buying hardware, rewriting applications, or changing operations. Relocate also covers moving resources to a different VPC, Region, or account. Rehost moves individual servers onto Amazon EC2 instances instead."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to break its monolithic application into microservices that use AWS Lambda and Amazon DynamoDB so it can add features quickly and scale each part independently. Which migration strategy is this?","o":["Refactor","Replatform","Rehost","Repurchase"],"a":[0],"e":"Refactor (re-architect) changes the application's architecture to use cloud-native features, often for agility and scale that the current design cannot achieve. Replatform makes only small optimizations without changing the core architecture."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's data center lease expires in three months, and it must move hundreds of servers to AWS before then. Which migration strategy is usually the fastest way to meet this deadline?","o":["Refactor every application into microservices","Rehost the servers to Amazon EC2","Repurchase every application as SaaS","Retain all applications on premises"],"a":[1],"e":"Rehost (lift and shift) moves applications without changes, making it the fastest strategy for large migrations on a deadline; applications can be optimized later. Refactoring requires significant development time and would not fit a three-month window."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company moves its Java web application from an on-premises application server to AWS Elastic Beanstalk with only minor configuration changes, so AWS handles capacity provisioning, load balancing, and platform patching. Which migration strategy is this?","o":["Rehost","Refactor","Replatform","Relocate"],"a":[2],"e":"Replatform (lift, tinker, and shift) moves an application to a managed platform with minimal changes to gain cloud benefits without re-architecting. Rehost would move the server as-is to Amazon EC2, keeping all platform management with the company."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which migration strategies move an application to AWS without making any changes to the application itself? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Refactor","Rehost","Repurchase","Relocate","Retire"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Rehost moves servers as-is to Amazon EC2, and Relocate moves workloads at the hypervisor level without application changes. Refactor re-architects the application, Repurchase replaces it with a different product, and Retire decommissions it."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which migration strategies result in an application NOT being moved to AWS during the current migration? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Retire","Replatform","Rehost","Refactor","Retain"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Retire decommissions the application and Retain keeps it in the source environment, so neither moves it to AWS now. Replatform, Rehost, and Refactor all move the application to AWS in different ways."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which migration strategy typically requires the most time and effort but gives the greatest ability to use cloud-native features?","o":["Rehost","Relocate","Retain","Refactor"],"a":[3],"e":"Refactor re-architects the application, which is the most effort but unlocks cloud-native capabilities such as serverless and microservices. Rehost is the fastest and least effort but keeps the existing architecture."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company is migrating an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition. Which tool converts the database schema and stored procedures to the target engine?","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS Application Migration Service","Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration"],"a":[1],"e":"For heterogeneous migrations, AWS SCT (or DMS Schema Conversion) converts the schema and code objects to the new engine, and AWS DMS then moves the data. DataSync transfers files and objects, not database schemas."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company is migrating an on-premises Microsoft SQL Server database to Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition with minimal downtime. Which AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","Amazon Inspector","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)"],"a":[2,4],"e":"In a heterogeneous migration, AWS SCT converts the schema to the new engine and AWS DMS migrates the data while the source stays online. Application Migration Service rehosts whole servers and does not convert database engines."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company is migrating an on-premises MySQL database to Amazon RDS for MySQL. Which statement about this migration is correct?","o":["It is a homogeneous migration, so AWS DMS can move the data without a schema conversion step","It requires AWS SCT because the target is a managed service","It must be done offline because AWS DMS cannot keep the source running","It requires shipping the data on a physical device"],"a":[0],"e":"When the source and target engines are the same, the migration is homogeneous and no schema conversion is needed, so AWS DMS can migrate the data directly. Schema conversion is only needed when the engines differ, not because the target is managed."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Before cutting over, a company wants its new Amazon RDS database to stay continuously in sync with its on-premises source database for several weeks while applications are tested. Which capability supports this?","o":["AWS DataSync scheduled file transfers","Manual nightly database exports copied to Amazon S3","AWS Database Migration Service ongoing replication using change data capture (CDC)","AWS Application Migration Service test launches"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS DMS can perform a full load and then continuously replicate changes using change data capture, keeping the target in sync until cutover. Nightly exports would leave the target up to a day out of date."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to lift and shift hundreds of physical and virtual servers to AWS using continuous block-level replication, non-disruptive test launches, and a short cutover window. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","AWS DataSync","AWS Backup","AWS Application Migration Service"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Application Migration Service is the primary AWS service for rehosting: it continuously replicates source servers at the block level and launches them as Amazon EC2 instances. AWS DMS migrates databases, not entire servers."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company needs to copy millions of files from an on-premises NFS file server to Amazon EFS over the network, with scheduling, encryption in transit, and data integrity validation. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS DataSync automates and accelerates online data transfer between on-premises storage (NFS, SMB, HDFS, object storage) and AWS storage services such as Amazon S3, EFS, and FSx. Application Migration Service replicates whole servers, not file shares."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"After migrating to Amazon RDS, a company wants a copy of its database in another AWS Region to serve local read traffic and to support disaster recovery. What should the company use?","o":["A Multi-AZ standby in the same Region","A cross-Region read replica","An Amazon EBS snapshot kept in the same Availability Zone","A larger DB instance class"],"a":[1],"e":"A cross-Region read replica asynchronously replicates data to another Region, where it can serve reads and be promoted for disaster recovery. A Multi-AZ standby stays in the same Region and does not serve read traffic in the standard configuration."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to continuously replicate changes from its on-premises PostgreSQL database into an Amazon S3 data lake for analytics, without stopping the source database. Which AWS service is designed for this?","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","AWS Storage Gateway"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS DMS supports Amazon S3 as a target and can capture ongoing changes from a running source database. DataSync copies files and objects but does not read database transaction changes."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company is building a total cost of ownership (TCO) comparison between its data center and AWS. Which on-premises costs are commonly underestimated or left out? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon EC2 On-Demand charges","Power and cooling for the server room","AWS Support plan fees","Data center real estate and physical security","Data transfer out from AWS to the internet"],"a":[1,3],"e":"On-premises TCO must include indirect costs such as power, cooling, real estate, and physical security, not just server prices. EC2 charges, AWS Support fees, and AWS data transfer are AWS-side costs, not on-premises costs."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A CFO compares only the purchase price of on-premises servers with Amazon EC2 prices and concludes that on-premises is cheaper. What is the main flaw in this comparison?","o":["It ignores indirect costs such as power, cooling, facilities, staff time, hardware refresh, and capacity that sits idle","EC2 prices include the cost of the customer's application licenses in all cases","On-premises servers never need to be replaced","AWS charges a fixed fee for every Region a company could use"],"a":[0],"e":"A fair TCO comparison includes all costs: facilities, power, cooling, labor, maintenance, hardware refresh cycles, and overprovisioned capacity. EC2 prices do not automatically include application licenses, so that is not the flaw."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which of these is an example of a variable cost for a company running workloads on AWS?","o":["A 10-year lease on a data center building","Servers purchased upfront and depreciated over five years","Charges for the Amazon EC2 instance-hours actually used each month","A fixed annual maintenance contract for on-premises storage arrays"],"a":[2],"e":"Variable costs change with consumption, like paying for the EC2 hours you actually use. Building leases, upfront server purchases, and fixed maintenance contracts are fixed costs paid regardless of usage."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"An on-premises data center was sized for the company's annual peak demand, so most servers are idle the rest of the year. Which cloud capability most directly removes this cost?","o":["Purchasing larger instances to handle every possible spike","Elastic scaling that adds capacity during peaks and removes it afterward","Moving the same number of servers to Dedicated Hosts","Running every workload in a single Availability Zone"],"a":[1],"e":"Elastic scaling matches capacity to demand, so you stop paying for idle capacity sized for the peak. Buying larger instances for every spike recreates the same overprovisioning problem in the cloud."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company reviews its Amazon EC2 instances and finds that many average under 10% CPU and memory utilization for a month. It switches them to smaller instance types. What is this practice called?","o":["Rehosting","Overprovisioning","Refactoring","Rightsizing"],"a":[3],"e":"Rightsizing matches instance types and sizes to actual workload needs to lower cost. Overprovisioning is the opposite: paying for more capacity than the workload uses."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company already owns Microsoft SQL Server licenses with License Mobility through Software Assurance and wants to use them for workloads on AWS instead of paying for new licenses. Which licensing approach is this?","o":["License included","Bring your own license (BYOL)","Pay-per-request licensing","Free tier licensing"],"a":[1],"e":"BYOL lets you use eligible licenses you already own on AWS, avoiding paying for the license again. With license-included pricing, the license cost is built into the hourly price, so the company would pay twice."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A startup has no Windows Server licenses and does not want to manage license purchases. It wants to run Windows on Amazon EC2 and pay for the license as part of the hourly instance price. Which licensing model is this?","o":["License included","Bring your own license (BYOL)","Dedicated Host licensing","Enterprise Agreement licensing"],"a":[0],"e":"With license-included instances, the Windows Server license cost is part of the instance price, so no separate license purchase is needed. BYOL requires the company to already own eligible licenses."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to bring existing software licenses that are bound to physical cores and sockets, so it needs visibility into and control over the physical server that runs its instances. Which Amazon EC2 option supports this?","o":["Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances on shared hardware","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts","AWS Lambda functions"],"a":[2],"e":"Dedicated Hosts give you a physical server dedicated to your use, with visibility into sockets and cores for server-bound BYOL licensing. Spot and shared On-Demand Instances run on shared hardware without that physical visibility."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company replaces manual, ticket-based server builds with automated provisioning using AWS CloudFormation. Which economic benefit is this change most likely to produce?","o":["All AWS charges for the provisioned resources are waived","Reserved Instances are purchased automatically","Application performance is guaranteed to improve","Lower labor costs and fewer costly human errors, because environments are built quickly and consistently"],"a":[3],"e":"Automation reduces manual work and configuration mistakes, lowering operational cost and speeding delivery. CloudFormation itself is free for AWS resource types, but the resources it creates are still billed."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company runs its own database on Amazon EC2, and its database administrators spend most of their time on backups, patching, and failover setup. Which change most reduces this operational overhead?","o":["Move to a larger EC2 instance","Migrate the database to Amazon RDS","Attach more Amazon EBS volumes","Move the database to an EC2 Dedicated Host"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon RDS is a managed service that automates backups, patching, and Multi-AZ failover, freeing staff for higher-value work. A larger or dedicated EC2 instance still leaves all database administration with the company."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"After migrating from its own data center to AWS, which tasks does a company's IT team no longer need to perform? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Configuring IAM permissions for its users","Replacing failed physical server hardware","Classifying its own data","Writing and testing its application code","Managing physical security of the data center"],"a":[1,4],"e":"AWS takes over physical infrastructure work such as hardware replacement and data center physical security. Under the shared responsibility model, the customer still manages IAM permissions, data classification, and its own application code."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company's on-premises servers reach end of life every four years, requiring a large capital purchase and a disruptive hardware refresh project. How does moving to AWS change this?","o":["The company must buy replacement hardware and ship it to AWS","AWS charges a separate refresh fee every four years","AWS manages and refreshes the underlying hardware, so the company avoids these capital purchases","Hardware refresh becomes the customer's responsibility in every AWS Region"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS owns and refreshes the physical infrastructure, so customers avoid periodic capital purchases and refresh projects. Customers never buy or ship their own hardware to AWS data centers for standard services."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A new startup builds and runs all of its applications in AWS and has no data center of its own. Which cloud deployment model is this?","o":["Hybrid","On-premises","Private cloud","Cloud (all-in cloud)"],"a":[3],"e":"In the cloud deployment model, all parts of the application run in the cloud, either built there or fully migrated. Hybrid would connect cloud resources to infrastructure the company still runs on premises."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company uses virtualization and resource management tools to run all workloads in its own data center and does not use any public cloud provider. Which deployment model is this?","o":["Hybrid","On-premises (private cloud)","Cloud (all-in cloud)","Multi-Region"],"a":[1],"e":"On-premises deployment, sometimes called private cloud, runs resources in the company's own data center using virtualization and management tools. Hybrid requires a connection between on-premises infrastructure and a cloud provider."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants to run AWS infrastructure and services inside its own on-premises facility for low-latency processing, while using the same APIs and tools as in the AWS Regions. Which AWS service supports this hybrid approach?","o":["AWS Outposts","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Local Zones","AWS Direct Connect"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Outposts brings AWS infrastructure, services, and APIs into the customer's own data center for a consistent hybrid experience. Local Zones are AWS-operated locations near cities, not equipment inside the customer's facility."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which are common reasons a company might choose a hybrid deployment model? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Regulations require certain data to remain in the company's own data center","To avoid paying any charges to AWS","To gain physical access to AWS hardware","A gradual multi-year migration in which on-premises and cloud systems must keep working together","Because AWS cannot run Windows workloads"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Hybrid is common when some data or systems must stay on premises for regulatory reasons, or during a phased migration. Hybrid still incurs AWS charges, and customers never get physical access to AWS hardware."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company wants the most control over the operating system, networking, and storage configuration of its servers while avoiding buying physical hardware. Which cloud computing model fits best?","o":["Software as a Service (SaaS)","Platform as a Service (PaaS)","Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)","On-premises deployment"],"a":[2],"e":"IaaS, such as Amazon EC2, provides virtual servers, networking, and storage with the highest level of control over IT resources. PaaS removes the need to manage the operating system, which reduces control."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Developers want to upload their application code and have the provider handle capacity provisioning, load balancing, scaling, and operating system patching, so they can focus on the application. Which cloud computing model is this?","o":["Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)","Software as a Service (SaaS)","Hybrid deployment","Platform as a Service (PaaS)"],"a":[3],"e":"PaaS, such as AWS Elastic Beanstalk, removes the need to manage the underlying infrastructure and operating system so you focus on deploying and managing your application. SaaS delivers a complete application you only use, not one you build."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company subscribes to a web-based email and calendar application. The vendor runs and maintains everything, and the company only configures user accounts and settings. Which cloud computing model is this?","o":["Software as a Service (SaaS)","Platform as a Service (PaaS)","Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)","On-premises deployment"],"a":[0],"e":"SaaS delivers a complete product that the provider runs and manages; the customer only uses and configures it. With PaaS, the customer still writes and manages its own application code."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"Which AWS service is the best example of Platform as a Service (PaaS)?","o":["Amazon EC2","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon VPC","Amazon EBS"],"a":[1],"e":"Elastic Beanstalk deploys and manages applications for you, handling capacity, load balancing, scaling, and platform updates, which is PaaS. Amazon EC2 is IaaS because you manage the operating system and everything above it."},
  {"d":1,"t":"concepts","q":"A company runs its application on Amazon EC2 instances, which is an Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) model. Which task remains the company's responsibility?","o":["Replacing failed physical hosts","Maintaining the hypervisor","Patching the guest operating system","Cooling the data center"],"a":[2],"e":"With IaaS, the customer manages the guest operating system, including patches, as well as applications and data. AWS manages the physical hosts, hypervisor, and facilities."}
];
